# {{lb|en|countable}}
## {{senseid|en|amount of space}} An [[amount#Noun|amount]] of [[space#Noun|space]] between [[point#Noun|points]] (often [[geographical#Adjective|geographical]] points), usually (but not [[necessarily]]) [[measure#Verb|measured]] along a [[straight line]].
##* {{RQ:Hakluyt Principall Navigations|author=w:Arthur Barlowe|chapter=The First Voyage Made to the Coastes of America, with Two Barkes, wherein were Captaines Master {{w|Philip Amadas}}, and Master Arthur Barlowe, who Discouered Part of the Countrey, Now Called Virginia, Anno 1584. Written by One of the Said Captaines, and Sent to Sir {{w|Walter Raleigh}}, Knight, at whose Charge and Direction, the Said Voyage was Set foorth.|year=1584|page=731|passage=[S]he {{...}} gaue vs into our boate our ſupper halfe dreſſed, pots, and all, and brought vs to our boates ſide, in which wee laye all night, remoouing the ſame a pretie '''diſtance''' from the ſhoare: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2 Q1|act=IV|scene=i|sig=G2|page=61|passage=The prince is here at hand, pleaſeth your Lordſhip / To meet his grace iuſt '''diſtance''' tvveene our armies.}}
##* {{RQ:Herbert Travaile|chapter=[Holy Port]|page=3|passage=The third of ''Aprill'', early in the morning, vvee had ſight of the ''Holy Port'' {{quote-gloss|[[Porto Santo]]}}, belonging to the ''Spaniard'', vvhich Ile at eight leagues '''diſtance''', gaue it ſelfe in this ſhape vnto vs.}}
##* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|page=4|passage=Novv by this time the Man vvas got a good '''diſtance''' from them; But hovvever they vvere reſolved to purſue him; vvhich they did and in little time they over-took him<!--. missing-->}}
##* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|chapter=Of Simple Modes; and First, of the Simple Modes of Space|sections=2–3|page=75|passage=[W]e get the ''Idea'' of Space, both by our Sight, and Touch; vvhich, I think, is ſo evident, that it vvould be as needleſs, to go to prove, that Men perceive by their Sight, a '''diſtance''' betvveen Bodies of different Colours, or betvveen the parts of the ſame Body; as that they ſee Colours themſelves: Nor is it leſs obvious, that they can do ſo in the Dark by Feeling and Touch. This Space conſidered barely in length betvveen any tvvo Beings, vvithout conſidering any thing elſe betvveen them, is called '''''diſtance''''': If conſidered in Length, Breadth, and Thickneſs, I think, it may be called ''Capacity'': {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Dampier New Voyage|chapter=VIII|page=225|passage=VVe kept at a good '''diſtance''' off ſhore, and ſavv no Land till the 14th day; but then, being in lat. 12 d. 50 m. the Volcan of ''Guatimala'' appeared in ſight.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian 1713|issue=167|page=2|column=1|passage=''Helim'' had placed tvvo of his ovvn Mules at about a Mile's '''Diſtance''' from the black Temple, on the Spot vvhich they had agreed upon for their Rendezvous.}}
##* {{RQ:Shelley Queen Mab|note=I. Page 13|page=125|passage=[O]bservations on the eclipses of Jupiter's satellites have demonstrated that light takes up no more than 8′ 7″ in passing from the sun to the earth, a '''distance''' of 95,000,000 miles.}}
##* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=VIII|pages=53–54|pageref=54|passage=Then everybody once more knelt, and soon the blessing was pronounced. The choir and the clergy trooped out slowly, through the open screen, down the nave to the western door. {{...}} At a seemingly immense '''distance''' the surpliced group stopped to say the last prayer.}}
##* {{RQ:Maugham Razor's Edge|section=iv|page=355|passage=I have never been to Dallas, but I suppose that, like other American cities I know, it has a residential district within easy motoring '''distance''' of the business section and the country club where the affluent have fine houses in large gardens with a handsome view of hill or dale from the living-room windows.}}
### {{lb|en|horse racing}} ''Chiefly in'' '''by a distance''': a space of more than 30 [[length#Noun|lengths]] (about 80 [[yard#Noun|yards]] or 7.3 [[metre#Noun|metres]]) between [[two#Numeral|two]] [[racehorse]]s [[finish#Verb|finishing]] a [[race#Noun|race]], [[use#Verb|used]] to [[describe]] the [[margin of victory]]; also {{lb|en|archaic}}, any space of 240 yards (about 219.5 [[metre#Noun|metres]]) on a [[racecourse]].
## ''Chiefly in'' '''from a distance''': a [[place#Noun|place]] which is [[far away#Adjective|far away]] or [[remote#Adjective|remote]]; specifically {{lb|en|especially|painting}}, a more remote [[part#Noun|part]] of a [[landscape#Noun|landscape]] or [[view#Noun|view]] as [[contrast#Verb|contrasted]] with the [[foreground#Noun|foreground]].
##* {{RQ:Berkeley Vision|section=LXXII|sectionname=Objection Answer'd|page=79|passage=VVhen from a '''Diſtance''' (I ſpeak vvith the Vulgar) vve behold great Objects, the Particles of the intermediate Air and Vapours, vvhich are themſelves unperceivable, do interrupt the Rays of Light, and thereby render the Appearance leſs Strong and Vivid; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Cowper Poems|poem=The Progress of Error|page=51|passage=Vievv'd from a '''diſtance''', and vvith heedleſs eyes, / Folly and innocence are ſo alike, / The diff'rence, though eſſential, fails to ſtrike.}}
##* {{RQ:Irving Sketch Book|title=Rip Van Winkle|page=71|passage=As he was about to descend, he heard a voice from a '''distance''', hallooing, "Rip Van Winkle! Rip Van Winkle!"}}
##* {{RQ:Darwin et al Voyages|volume=III|chapter=I|page=8|passage=The rocks of St. Paul appear from a '''distance''' of a brilliantly white colour. This is partly owing to the dung of a vast multitude of seafowl, and partly to a coating of a glossy white substance, which is intimately united to the surface of the rocks.}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|pages=104–105|pageref=105|passage=And every man on board {{quote-gloss|the ship}}, waking or sleeping, good or bad, had had a kinder word for another on that day than on any day in the year; and had shared to some extent in its festivities; and had remembered those he cared for at a '''distance''', and had known that they delighted to remember him.}}
##* {{RQ:Kingsley Hereward the Wake|volume=I|chapter=Prelude. Of the Fens.|page=18|passage=Dark and sad were those short autumn days, when all the '''distances''' were shut off, and the air choked with foul brown fog and drenching rains from off the eastern sea; {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-song|en|author=w:Julie Gold|artist=w:Nanci Griffith|title=w:From a Distance|album=w:Lone Star State of Mind|location=Universal City, Calif.|publisher=w:MCA Records|year='''1985''' (date written)|year_published=1987|oclc=15279458|passage=From a '''distance''', you look like my friend / Even though we are at war / From a '''distance''', I just cannot comprehend / What all this fighting's for // {{...}} // God is watching us / From a '''distance'''}}
