##* {{RQ:Burnet History|volume=I|pages=226–227|pageref=227|passage=The buſy men in ''Scotland'', being encouraged from ''Rotterdam'', vvent about the country, to try if any men of vveight vvould ſet themſelves at the head of their deſigns for an inſurrection. The Earl of ''Caſſilis'' {{quote-gloss|''i.e.'', {{w|John Kennedy, 6th Earl of Cassilis}}}} and ''Lockhart'' {{quote-gloss|{{w|William Lockhart of Lee}}}} vvere the tvvo perſons they reſolved to try. But they did it at ſo great a '''diſtance''', that, from the propoſition made to them, there vvas no danger of miſpriſion of treaſon.}}
