# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## ''Often followed by'' '''[[from]]''': to [[set#Verb|set]] (someone or something) at a [[distance#Noun|distance]] ''(noun {{senseno|en|amount of space}})'' from someone or something else.
##* {{RQ:Fuller Iniured Innocence|part=II|page=6|passage=If therefore the ''Interpoſition'' of ''Gloceſterſhire'' '''diſtanceth''' ''VVorceſterſhire'' from ''confining'' on the ''VVeſt-Saxons'', the ''Animadvertor'' ought to have vented his diſpleaſure not on ''Me'', but on ''{{w|Bede}}'', and [[w:Henry of Huntingdon|{{quote-gloss|Henry of}} ''Huntington'']], vvhoſe vvords I exactly tranſlated.}}
##* {{RQ:Fuller Worthies of England|part=2|chapter=Lancashire|page=106|passage=The faireſt {{quote-gloss|oxen}} in ''England'' are bred (or if you vvill, made) in this County, vvith goodly heads, the Tips of vvhoſe horns are ſometimes '''diſtanced''' ''five'' foot aſunder.}}
##* {{RQ:Emerson Conduct of Life|page=22|passage=It {{quote-gloss|an insight}} '''distances''' those who share it, from those who share it not.}}
## To [[cause#Verb|cause]] (a [[place#Noun|place]], a [[thing]], etc.) to [[seem]] [[distant]], or {{lb|en|figurative}} [[unfamiliar#Adjective|unfamiliar]].
##* {{RQ:Du Fresnoy Dryden Painting|chapter=Observations|page=170|passage=That vvhich gives the Relievo to a Bovvl, (may ſome ſay to me) is the quick Light, or the vvhite, vvhich appears to be on the ſide, vvhich is neareſt to us, and the black by conſequence '''diſtances''' the Object: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Lowell Fireside Travels|page=191|passage=Below you, where the valley widens greenly toward other mountains, which the ripe Italian air '''distances''' with a bloom like that on unplucked grapes, are more arches, ossified arteries of what was once the heart of the world.}}
## To [[leave behind]] (someone or something [[move#Verb|moving]] in the same [[direction]]; specifically, other [[competitor]]s in a [[race#Noun|race]]) some distance away; to [[outpace]], to [[outstrip]].
##: {{synonyms|en|outdistance|outgallop<q:chiefly of a horse or its rider>|outrun}}
##* {{RQ:Longfellow Golden Legend|page=238|passage=Our fleeter steeds have '''distanced''' our attendants; / They lag behind us with a slower pace; / We will await them under the green pendants / Of the great willows in this shady place.}}
##* {{RQ:Craddock Stranger People's Country|chapter=V|page=120|passage=He heard only here and there the ecstatic burst of a mocking-bird's wonderful roulades. Then the horse, with muscles as strong as steel, '''distanced''' the sound.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### To [[keep#Verb|keep]] (someone) [[emotionally]] or [[socially]] [[apart]] from another [[person#Noun|person]] or people.
### To [[exceed]] or [[surpass]] (someone, such as a [[peer#Noun|peer]] or [[rival#Noun|rival]]); to [[outdo]], to outstrip.
### {{lb|en|reflexive}} To keep (oneself) away from someone or something, especially because one does not [[want#Verb|want]] to be [[associate#Verb|associated]] with that person or thing.
###: {{ux|en|He '''distanced''' himself from the comments made by some of his colleagues.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=Daniel Burston|title={{lang|grc|ΈΥΑΓΓΕΛΙΣΤΗΣ ἐτι ΈΥΑΓΓΕΛΙΖΟΜΕΝΟΣ}} [ÉUAGGELISTES eti ÉUAGGELIZOMENOS] or, The Evangelist yet Evangelizing.{{nb...|Submitted to the Judgment and Censure of the Churches of England and Ireland.}}|location=Dublin|publisher={{...|Printed by}} John Crook,{{nb...|printer to the Kings Most Excellent Majestie}}, and are to be sold by Samuel Dancer,{{nb...|bookseller in Castlestreet}}|year=1662|page=41|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6X5mAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA41|oclc=The Evangelist yet Evangelizing|passage=[S]ince the Church hath, and ought to have a Government, it muſt not only be inoffenſive, but moſt proper, to call it an Hierarchy, or holy Government; {{...}} Beſides, [[w:John Calvin|{{quote-gloss|John}} C''alvin'']] admitting of ''ſacrum regimen'' {{quote-gloss|holy government}}, over nicely '''diſtanceth''' himſelf from thoſe vvho call it ''Hierarchy'', for he ſaith the ſame in Latin, vvhich they do in Greek; {{...}}}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=Abby Green|chapter=Prologue|title=A Christmas Bride for the King|location=London|publisher=w:Mills & Boon|year=2017|page=7|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/christmasbridefo0000gree_n2i9/page/7/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-263-92494-7|passage=[H]e'd built his life around an independence he'd cultivated as far back as he could remember. '''Distancing''' himself from his own family and the heavy legacy of his birth. '''Distancing''' himself from painful memories. '''Distancing''' himself from emotional entanglements or investment, which could only lead to unbearable heartbreak.}}
###* {{quote-journal|en|author=Philip Haigh|title=TPE Must Choose the Right Route to a Brighter Future|journal=[[w:Rail (magazine)|Rail]]|location=Peterborough, Cambridgeshire|publisher=[[w:Bauer Media Group|Bauer Media]]|date=1 November 2023|issue=995|page=57|issn=0953-4563|oclc=999467860|passage=But Gisby '''distances''' himself from calling TPE an inter-city operator.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|US|horse racing|archaic}} Of a [[racehorse]]: to [[beat#Verb|beat]] (another [[horse#Noun|horse]]) by a certain distance; also {{lb|en|passive voice}}, to cause (a horse) to be [[disqualify|disqualified]] by beating it by a certain distance.
##* {{RQ:Guardian 1713|issue=6|page=2|column=1|passage=[H]e is of Opinion it is inhuman, that Animals ſhould be put upon their utmoſt Strength and Metal for our Diverſion only. Hovvever, not to be particular, he puts in for the Queen's Plate every Year, vvith Orders to his Rider never to vvin or be '''diſtanced'''; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}}
### To [[cover#Verb|cover]] the [[entire#Adjective|entire]] distance to (something).
###* {{RQ:More Psychodia|chapter=Psychathanasia|book=3|canto=1|stanza=21|page=61|passage=The ſunne and all the ſtarres that do appear / She {{quote-gloss|Psyche}} feels them in herſelf, can '''diſtance''' all, / For ſhe is at each one purely preſentiall.}}
### To [[depart#Verb|depart]] from (a place); to [[leave#Verb|leave]] (a place) behind.
###* {{quote-book|en|author=&#91;{{w|Elizabeth Charles}}&#93;|chapter=VII|title=Against the Stream: The Story of an Heroic Age in England{{nb...|by the Author of “The Schonberg-Cotta Family”: In Three Vols.}}|location=London|publisher=Strahan & Co.{{nb...|56, Ludgate Hill, London}}|year=1873|volume=I|page=139|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/againststreamsto01char/page/139/mode/1up|oclc=52997726|passage=[W]e heard the joyous voices sound louder and freer as they '''distanced''' the solemn precincts, scattering frolic and music through the town as they separated to their different homes.}}
### To [[indicate]] or [[measure#Verb|measure]] the distance to (a place).
###* {{RQ:Fuller Palestine|chapter=How the Hebrews Measured Places. Of Their Cubits, Furlongs, Miles and Sabbath-days-journeys.|paragraph=1|page=40|passage=THe Hebrevvs '''diſtanced''' their places by ſeverall meaſures, ſome arbitrary, caſuall, and uncertain; others certain, as reduced to a conſtant ſtandard. Of the former vvas their meaſuring of land by paces, for vve read, vvhen ''{{w|David}}'' ſolemnly brought the Ark into ''Jeruſalem'', ''vvhen he had gone ſix paces he offered oxen and fatlings''.}}
### To set ([[two#Numeral|two]] or more things) at [[regular#Adjective|regular]] distances from each other; to [[space#Verb|space]], to [[space out]].
###* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Giacomo Leoni|chapter=Of the Corinthian Order|translator=Nicholas Du Bois|title=The Architecture of [[w:Andrea Palladio|A[ndrea] Palladio]];{{nb...|in Four Books. Containing, a Short Treatise of the Five Orders, and the Most Necessary Observations Concerning All Sorts of Building, as also the Different Construction of Private and Publick Houses, High-ways, Bridges, Market-places, Xystes, and Temples, with Their Plans, Sections, and Upright. To which are Added Several Notes and Observations Made by Inigo Jones, Never Printed before. Revis’d, Design’d, and Publish’d by Giacomo Leoni, a Venetian; Architect to His Most Serene Highness, the Elector Palatine. Translated from the Italian Original.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} John Watts, for the author|year=1715|page=30|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-architecture-of-a-p_palladio-andrea_1715_1/page/n67/mode/1up|oclc=1326263064|passage=In the deſign of a ''Colonnade'', or ſingle Columns, the ''Inter-columns'' are tvvo diameters, as in the ''Portico'' of St. ''Maria Rotunda'' at ''Rome''; and this manner of '''''diſtancing''''' the Columns is, by ''{{w|Vitruvius}}'', call'd ''Syſtylos''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|reflexive}} ''Often followed by'' '''from'''.
## To set oneself at a distance from someone or something else; to move away from someone or something.
## {{lb|en|figurative}} To keep oneself emotionally or socially apart from another person or people; to [[keep one's distance]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=David S. Freeman|chapter=The Major Systems Involved in the Family Therapy Process|title=Multigenerational Family Therapy|location=Binghamton, N.Y.&#59; London|publisher=[[w:Haworth Press|The Haworth Press]]|year=1992|page=66|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/multigenerationa0000free/page/66/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-56024-125-6|passage=When a family member goes outside of the family to deal with a problem, he or she '''distances''' from the family. The family therapist offers the family the opportunity to deal with problems in a way that will allow them to deepen their connections with each other. The therapist will not be able to accomplish this goal if various family members go outside the family to resolve their problems.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Goran Arbanas|chapter=Anxiety and Somatoform Disorders|editors=Michal Lew-Starowicz; Annamaria Giraldi; Tillman H. C. Krüger|title=Psychiatry and Sexual Medicine: A Comprehensive Guide for Clinical Practitioners|location=Cham, Zug, Switzerland|publisher=w:Springer Nature|year=2021|page=272|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=3PD6DwAAQBAJ&pg=PA272|doi=10.1007/978-3-030-52298-8|isbn=978-3-030-52297-1|passage=Also, due to irritability, tension, startle reactions, and feelings of no future love and enjoyment, the partners '''distance''' one from another and stop doing things together. {{...}} The therapeutic relationship with PTSD {{quote-gloss|post-traumatic stress disorder}} patients is very difficult at the beginning of the therapy as these patients will not allow the therapist "to come close to them"—for the same reason as they '''distance''' from their family members and partners (the therapist cannot understand them as she/he has not experienced the same thing they did and they are not good enough to be helped; they believe they destroy every person they come into contact with).}}

